WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.790 --> 00:00:05.390
The Friday the 13th series is one of the most
enduring mainstays in slasher cinema.

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:09.460
Sure, for decades, critics railed against
the saga of Jason Voorhees for being nothing

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:14.180
more than a shallow slaying delivery system,
cheap and hacky, yet audiences kept coming

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:15.180
back for more.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.670
Now, the series is so embedded in pop culture
that even people who've never seen the series

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:24.300
know the face of Jason Voorhees, but unless
they've sat with the whole saga, they only

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:25.900
know part of the story.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:31.470
Here are seven things even hardcore horror
fans may not know about Friday the 13th.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:32.470
Zero stars

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:37.590
Today, whole generations have grown up knowing
Friday the 13th as a classic series, but there

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:40.800
are likely still some critics who would scoff
to hear them say it.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.540
In fact, each entry was raked across the coals
by critics, with the most well-received movie,

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:49.900
the original, still topping out with a "rotten"
ranking by Rotten Tomatoes' reckoning.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:54.260
Many critics used the series as an excuse
to pen famously lacerating reviews.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:59.040
Gene Siskel found the original so distasteful
that he used his write-up to both spoil the

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:03.070
movie and encourage readers to write letters
to Paramount in protest of the film.

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:04.080
As the critic wrote,

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.300
"I hope I've ruined Friday the 13th, which
is the latest film by one of the most despicable

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.909
creatures ever to infest the movie business.

00:01:10.909 --> 00:01:14.490
[...] If any film should be X-rated on the
basis of violence, this is it."

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:17.500
"How does a guy like that go into a store
and buy a new hockey mask?

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.850
I mean what do you think he looks like underneath
the mask?"

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:22.450
"I know exactly what, I'd love to give away
the ending of this picture.

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:23.909
I'm debating it."

00:01:23.909 --> 00:01:28.420
Like the later slasher series Saw, Friday
the 13th never stopped doing very poorly with

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:31.030
critics while working just fine for audiences.

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:35.690
In Friday the 13th's case, critics almost
seemed vindictive, wondering how people could

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:40.020
enjoy and financially reward such a formulaic
series of violent trash.

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:43.770
You'd think they would've figured out by movie
six or seven that the violent trashiness was

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:44.860
the whole point.

00:01:47.229 --> 00:01:49.240
Televised tales of terror

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:55.580
Friday the 13th: The Series ran for 72 episodes
from 1987 to 1990, with Jason Voorhees and

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:59.640
Camp Crystal Lake showing up a grand total
of zero times.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:04.060
Originally developed under the name The 13th
Hour and released in some markets as Friday's

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:08.379
Curse, the series followed two cousins who
travel the world with an occultist researcher

00:02:08.379 --> 00:02:11.129
to collect magical or cursed relics.

00:02:11.129 --> 00:02:14.980
The open-ended story allowed for an episodic
series in the vein of Supernatural or The

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:19.060
X-Files, with one overarching plot connecting
standalone tales.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:23.489
It was Friday the 13th in name only, which
star John LeMay felt might have worked against

00:02:23.489 --> 00:02:24.709
the series in the long run.

00:02:24.709 --> 00:02:29.379
"I think a lot of people who were really big
fans of the series didn't like the fact that

00:02:29.379 --> 00:02:33.769
they stole the name and tried to sneak a fast
one past everybody."

00:02:33.769 --> 00:02:38.069
While the series is up-and-down in terms of
quality, it's associated with the earlier

00:02:38.069 --> 00:02:43.109
work of a few great filmmakers, with episodes
directed or appearances by the likes of David

00:02:43.109 --> 00:02:45.890
Cronenberg, Atom Egoyan, and Sarah Polley.

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:50.260
Not surprisingly, Friday the 13th was soon
joined on television by a Nightmare on Elm

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:53.189
Street spin-off series called Freddy's Nightmares.

00:02:53.189 --> 00:02:57.109
While that show didn't last as long, it did
at least have its famous slasher, Freddy Krueger,

00:02:57.109 --> 00:02:59.209
in the cast.

00:02:59.209 --> 00:02:59.800
[Evil laughter]

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:01.840
"But the game's gotta end!"

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:02.560
[Gunshot]

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:03.560
"Arrgh!"

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:07.520
The series that wouldn't end

00:03:07.522 --> 00:03:10.819
Movie series producer Frank Mancuso, Jr. was
so confident that the series was over after

00:03:10.819 --> 00:03:14.749
the 3-D third movie that he declared aloud
at the movie's wrap party, which celebrated

00:03:14.749 --> 00:03:19.650
Jason's demise with "Happy End Day" cake,
that he was not going to produce another one.

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:25.340
"When we got done with three, I was like,
'Okay, what I wanna do now is, I really wanna

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:28.049
bring this thing to an effective close.'"

00:03:28.049 --> 00:03:32.359
The decision was walked back after Part III
opened to the most successful opening weekend

00:03:32.359 --> 00:03:34.079
the series had seen yet.

00:03:34.079 --> 00:03:37.870
This time, they decided to be decisive with
it, giving the fourth entry the subtitle of

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:39.620
The Final Chapter.

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:43.260
This being a 12-film franchise, we all know
how that turned out.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:50.169
"Friday the 13th, 13 hour movie marathon continues
right here, only on the new TNN!"

00:03:50.169 --> 00:03:54.219
Even at the time, it seemed producers had
cold feet about truly ending Jason.

00:03:54.219 --> 00:03:58.359
Special effects designer Tom Savini and his
team of artists made several sculptures that

00:03:58.359 --> 00:04:02.930
showed Jason not just taking a machete to
the face, but being fully decapitated.

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:07.249
These effects, of course, went unused, so
Jason could live again, just in case.

00:04:07.249 --> 00:04:11.209
The series would ultimately end up having
two inaccurately-named last chapters: the

00:04:11.209 --> 00:04:15.019
fourth movie and the ninth, Jason Goes to
Hell: The Final Friday.

00:04:15.019 --> 00:04:17.350
Tommy Jarvis almost replaced Jason

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:21.640
Portrayed by three different actors in three
of the franchise's movies, Tommy Jarvis is

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:25.850
arguably the second-most important character
in the franchise, not that he makes a lot

00:04:25.850 --> 00:04:26.850
of sense.

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:30.470
Twice, the filmmakers ended a sequel with
the tease that Tommy was damned to become

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:35.010
the new Jason, possessed or corrupted by his
spirit, only for the concept to be walked

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:36.310
back each time.

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:39.850
The fifth movie appeared to end with Tommy
on the verge of madness, having been driven

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:42.480
insane by visions of the Crystal Lake slasher.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:46.230
The setup for a sixth movie with Tommy as
the new Jason could not have been clearer

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:50.510
or more explicit, which makes it all the more
jarring when the sixth movie begins with him

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:52.080
as a hero once again.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.750
Evidently, his insanity was cured off screen.

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:59.450
Not that we're complaining, of course, in
a franchise as shallow as Friday the 13th,

00:04:59.450 --> 00:05:03.100
the tragic story arc of a slasher, Tommy is
probably best left unexplored.

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:08.630
"I think they could've gone a great number
of really cool places with it after that point."

00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:10.090
Bring Jason back

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:14.280
When producers brought the series back for
part five, they remained curiously determined

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:15.631
to make the end of Jason stick.

00:05:15.631 --> 00:05:16.631
[Scary music]

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:21.100
[Grunting and even scarier music]

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:30.340
In Part 5: A New Beginning, the series tried
to have it both ways, keeping Jason dead while

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:34.540
bringing in a ruthless, hockey mask-wearing
copycat slayer to slay in his stead.

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:38.680
When the slasher is revealed to be a minor
character in the movie, audiences scratched

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:39.680
their heads.

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:44.530
"Don't allow the overweight, mentally handicapped
son of the local ambulance driver to get hacked

00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:47.430
to s--- over a candy bar, because chances
are that driver is gonna turn into a Jason

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:48.700
clone and try to take people out."

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:49.870
What's interesting about that?

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:54.370
"It remains one of the least satisfying endings
in the history of film as far as I'm concerned."

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:58.260
The failure to effectively switch up the formula
was acknowledged at the start of the next

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:02.711
movie's production, with a directive coming
down from producer Frank Mancuso, Jr. to bring

00:06:02.711 --> 00:06:05.200
Jason back for the sixth movie, no matter
what.

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:11.860
"Five was a bad dream, so it was important
to me that Jason come back in a very spectacular,

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:12.860
fun way."

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:15.030
A budget slashed to pieces

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:18.640
The most disappointing Friday is easily the
eighth one.

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:23.670
Subtitled Jason Takes Manhattan, the 1989
release is well-known for generally not taking

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:25.060
place in Manhattan.

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:29.630
Instead, the majority of the movie takes place
on a small boat slowly floating from the confines

00:06:29.630 --> 00:06:33.990
of Crystal Lake out to the edge of the Atlantic
Ocean, somehow.

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:38.800
This wasn't a creative decision, but rather
an inconvenient financial limitation.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:43.430
Many fantastic, NYC-centric setpieces were
planned for the movie, almost none of which

00:06:43.430 --> 00:06:46.240
were actually accomplished on the film's small
budget.

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:48.030
According to producer Rob Hedden,

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:51.320
"Everything about New York was going to be
completely exploited and milked.

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:55.200
There was going to be a tremendous scene on
the Brooklyn Bridge, a boxing match in Madison

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:58.889
Square Garden, Jason would go through department
stores, Broadway plays.

00:06:58.889 --> 00:07:01.830
He'd even crawl onto the top of the Statue
of Liberty and dive off."

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:07.520
"Production reality set in, and it was like,
okay, instead of, you know, uh, 75 percent

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:09.630
of the movie taking place in New York, we'll
do 50 percent.

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:12.120
And then it was, well we can't really do 50,
either."

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:16.550
To save money on location shooting, the production
shot most of the scarce Manhattan material

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:19.250
in the not-quite-lookalike city of Vancouver.

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:23.450
"So there was a joke running around, it was
'Jason Takes Vancouver,' instead of 'Jason

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:24.530
Takes Manhattan.'"

00:07:24.530 --> 00:07:26.220
A long-awaited duel

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:32.050
In 2003, the Nightmare on Elm Street and Friday
the 13th series met in a long-awaited collision:

00:07:32.050 --> 00:07:33.280
Freddy vs. Jason.

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:37.530
While the movie might strike the uninitiated
as a cynical way to wring one last payday

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:41.940
out of both franchises after they ran out
of gas, the first attempt to make it actually

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:46.780
took place in 1987, when the Elm Street series
was ascendant and Friday was just beginning

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:49.070
to see its box office fortunes decline.

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:53.060
At the time, both properties were owned by
different studios, meaning any crossover would

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:56.440
involve tricky negotiations that proved difficult
to navigate.

00:07:56.440 --> 00:08:00.750
Paramount owned Friday while New Line Cinema
owned Nightmare, and both parties wanted the

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:01.750
upper hand.

00:08:01.750 --> 00:08:05.531
As a result, progress on putting the movie
together was full of starts and stops, not

00:08:05.531 --> 00:08:10.880
coming together until after New Line became
the owner of both properties in 1989.

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:15.970
With the franchises united under New Line,
the crossover was teased at the end of 1993's

00:08:15.970 --> 00:08:19.660
Jason Goes to Hell: The Final Friday, with
Freddy's fist bursting from beneath the earth

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:22.230
to drag Jason's mask underground.

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:27.200
Even so, ten years would pass and two more
movies, Jason X and Wes Craven's New Nightmare,

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:30.010
would come and go before the fight flick finally
came together.

00:08:30.010 --> 00:08:33.659
Fortunately, after all that buildup, they
ended up sticking the landing, not only is

00:08:33.659 --> 00:08:38.029
Freddy vs. Jason a pretty fun watch, it's
also the highest-grossing movie in either

00:08:38.029 --> 00:08:39.579
series by far.

00:08:39.579 --> 00:08:41.719
Teamwork, that's how you go out on top.

00:08:41.719 --> 00:08:42.919
Will it ever happen again?

00:08:42.919 --> 00:08:43.919
Well…

00:08:43.919 --> 00:08:45.790
"There's room for more!"

